MINIMUM avoiding index at a = 1/5: 16   (witness HNF basis (4, 0, 4))
  witness sublattice has no vector with N in [4, 7, 9, 12, 13] ; its N values <= 60: [16, 48]
  [OK] coset colouring on this sublattice is proper on cell INTERIORS (not yet on the 1-skeleton)

Corrected derivation (proof, not search).  The Eisenstein norm N is multiplicative and
N(k*lambda) = k^2 N(lambda), so a sublattice containing lambda contains 2*lambda, 3*lambda, ...
Avoidance therefore needs N(lambda) and 4N(lambda) and 9N(lambda) all outside F_N.
At a = 1/5, F_N = {4,7,9,12,13}: n = 1 gives 4 in F_N, n = 3 gives 12 in F_N, and
n in {4,7,9,12,13} are themselves forbidden, so N(lambda) >= 16 for every nonzero lambda.
Packing bound: covolume >= (sqrt3/2)*min|lambda|^2, and index = covolume/((sqrt3/2)*3a^2)
  = min|lambda|^2/(3a^2) = min N  ->  index >= 16.
Attained by 4*T = 4Z x 4Z (index 16), whose norms are all multiples of 16: 0 forced offsets.

(The earlier heuristic '|lambda| > 1+D forces index >= ceil((1+D)^2/(3a^2)) = 17' was WRONG:
ring 16 has |lambda| = 1.3856 < 1+D = 1.4 and is unforced, because dmin >= 1 there (the two
cells are far apart in a direction where the hexagon is thin).  The bound is 16, not 17.)

CONTROL a = 2/5 (paper's 7-colouring): minimum avoiding index = 7; bound ceil((1.8)^2/(3*0.16))
  = 6.7500 -> >= 7.  Bound SHARP there, so the 7-colouring is the minimal-index
  periodic colouring at a = 2/5.
